INTERPRETATION BY BASE:

  b=2 (tournaments/graphs): (2/p) depends on p mod 8
  b=3 (3-coloring/oriented): (3/p) depends on p mod 12
  b=4 (digraphs):  (4/p) = (2/p)² = 1 ALWAYS → 4^C ≡ 1 mod p²
  b=5 (5-coloring): (5/p) depends on p mod 5 (QR)

b=4 COROLLARY: The labeled digraph count 4^{C(p,2)} ≡ 1 mod p² ALWAYS.
This means digraphs have NO QR discrimination at the labeled level.
This is because 4 = 2² is a PERFECT SQUARE — always a QR.

UNIVERSAL QR BASES (b/p) = 1 for all p):
  Perfect squares: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, ... → b^{C(p,2)} ≡ 1 mod p² always
  These give combinatorial structures with TRIVIAL QR resonance.

NON-TRIVIAL QR BASES:
  2: discriminates p mod 8
  3: discriminates p mod 12
  5: discriminates p mod 5
  6 = 2×3: (6/p) = (2/p)(3/p), discriminates p mod 24
  7: discriminates p mod 28
  ...etc. Nonsquare bases all create nontrivial QR resonance.

==============================================================================
PART 4: DIRICHLET CHARACTER CONNECTION
  The Legendre symbol χ(n) = (n/p) is a Dirichlet character mod p.
  THM-307 uses χ(2) = (2/p). What about the full L-function?
==============================================================================

The Euler quotient f_p = (2^{(p-1)/2} - (2/p))/p
encodes HOW DEEPLY 2 is/isn't a QR mod p.

Connection to class numbers (p ≡ 3 mod 4):
  h(-p) = class number of Q(√(-p))
  L(1, χ_{-p}) = π × h(-p) / √p

   p  p%4  (2/p)  h(-p)  f_p%p  T(p)%p  v_2(T)
--------------------------------------------------
   3    3     -1      0      1       2       1
   5    1     -1    n/a      1       2       2
   7    3      1      1      1       1       3
  11    3     -1      1      3       2       5
  13    1     -1    n/a      5       2       6
  17    1      1    n/a     15       2       8
  19    3     -1      1      8       3       9
  23    3      1      3     20       ?       ?

KEY OBSERVATION: h(-p) and T(p) mod p are INDEPENDENT invariants
of the same underlying object: the multiplicative group (Z/pZ)*.
h(-p) measures the DISTRIBUTION of QR in {1,...,(p-1)/2}.
T(p) mod p measures the DEPTH of 2's residuosity (Euler quotient).

DIRICHLET SERIES PERSPECTIVE:

  Define D(s) = Σ_{n≥1} T(n)/n^s (Dirichlet series).
  This doesn't converge classically, but we can study
  T(n) mod p^k for fixed p as a p-adic Dirichlet series.

  From THM-307: T(p) ≡ f_p - (2/p)w_p - A_p (mod p)
  The (2/p) = χ_8(p) (the character of conductor 8).
  So T(p) mod p is influenced by χ_8 — the QR character of 2.

  This means: the 'Dirichlet series mod p' of tournament counts
  has a component proportional to L(s, χ_8) at the prime level!

==============================================================================
PART 5: STATISTICAL MECHANICS — ISING ON K_n
==============================================================================


The Burnside counting formula IS a partition function.

SETUP: The Ising model on the complete graph K_n.
- Each EDGE gets a spin σ_e ∈ {+1, -1} (= arc orientation in tournament language)
- The "Hamiltonian" is H(config) = -Σ_{triangles} σ_a σ_b σ_c
  (penalizes frustrated triangles = directed 3-cycles)
- The partition function is Z(β) = Σ_configs exp(-β H)

At β = 0 (infinite temperature): Z = 2^{C(n,2)} = total configs.
The UNLABELED partition function is:
  Z_orb(β) = (1/n!) Σ_{σ ∈ S_n} Σ_configs fixed_by_σ exp(-β H(config))

At β = 0: Z_orb(0) = T(n) × n!/n! ... well, T(n) for tournaments.

THE v_2 THEOREM AS A THERMODYNAMIC STATEMENT:

  v_2(T(n)) = (n-1)/2 for odd n

means: the "combinatorial free energy" F = -log_2(T(n)) satisfies

  F ≡ (n-1)/2 (mod integer part)

More precisely: T(n) = 2^{(n-1)/2} × (odd number), so
  log_2(T(n)) = (n-1)/2 + log_2(odd part)

The fractional part of log_2(T(n)) at the first binary digit is EXACTLY
determined by the QR structure.

Thermodynamic table (odd n):
   n      log2(T)  (n-1)/2    log2(odd)  entropy/edge
-------------------------------------------------------
   3       1.0000      1.0       0.0000      0.333333
   5       3.5850      2.0       1.5850      0.358496
   7       8.8329      3.0       5.8329      0.420614
   9      17.5473      4.0      13.5473      0.487424
  11      29.7514      5.0      24.7514      0.540934
  13      45.4643      6.0      39.4643      0.582876
  15      64.7499      7.0      57.7499      0.616666
  17      87.6624      8.0      79.6624      0.644576
  19     114.2445      9.0     105.2445      0.668097

The entropy per edge → 1 as n → ∞ (almost all tournaments are 'random').
But the 2-adic constraint v_2 = (n-1)/2 persists at ALL n.
This is a ZERO-TEMPERATURE CONSTRAINT surviving at all temperatures.

ANTIFERROMAGNETIC CONNECTION:
  Ground state = max frustration = max c_3 = tournament with most 3-cycles
  The H-maximizer (Paley tournament at primes) is the antiferromagnetic ground state
  of the Ising model on K_p.

  v_2(T(p)) = |QR_p| says: the number of 'symmetry-distinct ground states'
  has 2-adic valuation = number of quadratic residues.
  This is a constraint on the DEGENERACY of the ground state manifold.

